"""
vc-mounter — VeraCrypt volume mounter with stable device identification.

Solves the /dev/sdb1-today-/dev/sde1-tomorrow problem: volumes are stored
in the config as /dev/disk/by-id/... paths (serial-number based, stable
across reboots and port changes) and resolved to the current /dev/sdXY
at mount time.

Security design:
  * Password and PIM entries use GtkPasswordEntryBuffer, which keeps the
    typed text in non-pageable (mlock'ed) memory where the platform allows
    and zeroes it on free; input-method caching/prediction is disabled.
  * The process is marked non-dumpable at startup: no core dumps, and no
    other process of the same user can read its memory (ptrace,
    /proc/PID/mem).
  * Limitation: to hand the password to the helper it has to pass through
    ordinary Python objects, which cannot be wiped. They live until the
    interpreter reuses that memory, so keep swap encrypted.
  * Everything that runs as root lives in a separate, root-owned helper
    (/usr/libexec/vc-mounter/vc-mounter-helper, installed by install.sh)
    with its own polkit action. This file runs entirely as the desktop
    user and contains no root code.
  * The password, PIM and KDF are sent to that helper once, over
    stdin only — never via pkexec's argv, the environment, or any file.
  * The helper answers VeraCrypt's own password and PIM prompts on stdin,
    so neither appears on any command line. A KDF chosen explicitly is
    passed as --hash=NAME and stays visible in the process list while the
    volume is mounted; "Autodetect" passes nothing.
  * One pkexec authentication mounts all checked volumes, in parallel.
  * Both entries are cleared and the KDF choice is reset after a fully
    successful mount, whenever the window is hidden to the tray, and on
    quit.
  * Nothing sensitive is written to the config file (device IDs only,
    file created 0600 in a 0700 directory).

Mapping tab:
  * "Mount all configured volumes" mounts everything connected and not yet
    mounted; "Dismount all" dismounts every VeraCrypt volume on the system.
  * A single volume is dismounted with the eject button at the end of its
    row, or by right-clicking the row. The right-click menu also offers
    "Force dismount…" once a normal dismount found the volume busy.

Tray / lifecycle:
  * Closing the window hides the app to a tray indicator (padlock icon;
    open padlock while volumes are mounted). Minimize behaves normally.
  * Tray menu: Show/hide, Mount volumes…, Dismount all, Quit.
  * "Start at login" checkbox (Config tab) installs
    ~/.config/autostart/vc-mounter.desktop launching with --hidden,
    so the app waits in the tray after logon.
  * The indicator is a small GTK3 AppIndicator program, vc-mounter-tray,
    kept next to this file and run as a subprocess (GTK4 has no tray API;
    GTK3+GTK4 cannot share a process). Requires:
        sudo apt install gir1.2-ayatanaappindicator3-0.1
    and GNOME's "Ubuntu AppIndicators" extension (enabled by default).
    Without it the app still works; closing the window then quits.

Requirements: Ubuntu with GTK4 + PyGObject (python3-gi, preinstalled on
GNOME), VeraCrypt 1.26.30 or newer (veracrypt CLI in /usr/bin), polkit
agent running (GNOME default), and the root helper installed once with
`sudo ./install.sh`.

Mount points: VeraCrypt defaults are used -> /media/veracrypt1..N.
"""

